# Shell

The interactive shell on this machine is fish; scripts are bash. They do not
share syntax, so always be explicit about which one a command is written for.

## Running commands

- Write ad-hoc commands in POSIX or bash syntax. Do not rely on fish-only
  constructs (`and`, `or`, `set -x`, `$status`, abbreviations) unless the
  command is explicitly meant for fish.
- The interactive fish config defines aliases, abbreviations, and wrapper
  functions, so a bare command name may not be the binary it looks like. When
  the exact program matters, call it as `command <tool>` or by absolute path.
- A `cd` does not carry over between separate commands. Use absolute paths, or
  chain the `cd` into the same command.

## Writing scripts

- Start every bash script with `#!/usr/bin/env bash`, and add
  `set -euo pipefail` unless the surrounding code already installs its own
  error handling.
- Quote every variable expansion, prefer `[[ ]]` over `[ ]`, and `$(...)`
  over backticks.
- Keep scripts shellcheck-clean and shfmt-formatted, and add a
  `# shellcheck source=` directive when sourcing a library by a computed path.
- Fish scripts live in `*.fish` files and use fish syntax throughout. Never
  mix bash syntax into them, or fish syntax into a bash script.
